#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163058} :activity/id #uuid "98dc20a6-b50c-5324-b83b-6ac8d56ecb70" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048491 :topic/id #uuid "a4553111-5fc5-5a4e-8071-e4805050c5b5" :topic/math-academy-id 3069} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163058 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163322 :tutorial/content "An important family of distributions in probability and statistics is the **student's $t$-distribution** (or $t$-**distribution**). The diagram below shows the probability density functions for some members of this family.\n\n![](images/08/08b2d66fda68155c6440568508dde075812a7fe0e7a855e1efe175df52830719.png)\n\nEach family member has an associated positive integer parameter $k,$ known as the **degrees of freedom**. \n\nThe probability density function of a student's $t$-distribution is symmetric and bell-shaped, similar to the standard normal distribution. However, $t$-distribution curves have fatter \"tails.\" A consequence is that extreme events (i.e., events far from the mean) are more likely with a $t$-distribution than a standard normal distribution.\n\nTo see how the student's $t$-distribution comes about, consider a large random sample of size $n$\n\n$$\nX_1, \\: X_2, \\: \\ldots, \\: X_{n}\n$$\n\n \n\ndrawn from a population with mean $\\mu$ and variance $\\sigma^2.$ According to the central limit theorem, \n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X}-\\mu}{\\sigma/\\sqrt{n}} \\approx N(0,1),\n$$\n\nwhere $\\overline{X}$ is the sample mean. \n\nIn reality, it's unlikely that the population variance $\\sigma^2$ is known, in which case it must be estimated. \n\nRecall that an unbiased estimator for $\\sigma^2,$ known as the sample variance, is given by\n\n$$\nS^2 = \\dfrac{1}{n-1} \\sum_{i=1}^n \\left( X_i - \\overline{X} \\right)^2.\n$$\n\nIf we estimate $\\sigma^2$ using $S^2,$ then it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\overline{X}-\\mu}{S/\\sqrt{n}} \\approx T_{n-1}\n$$\n\nwhere $T_{n-1}$ is a $t$-distributed random variable with $n-1$ degrees of freedom. \n\nThe $t$-distribution is widely used in statistical estimation problems. We'll learn more about the practical applications of this important distribution in future lessons." :tutorial/id #uuid "86069b59-4cfe-5965-9f08-bd7cbfca0b5f" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6250 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "9e43698a-bcd5-5e90-92a2-a01cfff08635" :step/math-academy-id 37900 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163059}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163059 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163323 :tutorial/content "When working with $t$-distributed random variables, we often want to find a value that gives rise to a specific probability. \n\nFor example, let $X\\sim T_{21}$ be a $t$-distributed random variable with $21$ degrees of freedom. Suppose we want to find the value of $x$ such that \n\n$$\nP(X\\geq x) = 0.01.\n$$\n\nIn other words, we wish to find the $x$-value corresponding to the shaded area shown below.\n\n![](images/3c/3c3ef041152494232ecb60408580965018132a024c406dcde0506ff519d7ed73.png)\n\nTo compute these probabilities, we use a **percentage points table**. A typical percentage points table for the student's $t$-distribution is shown below.\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ |\n| $20$ | $1.325$ | $1.725$ | $2.086$ | $2.528$ | $2.845$ |\n| $21$ | $1.323$ | $1.721$ | $2.080$ | $2.518$ | $2.831$ |\n| $22$ | $1.321$ | $1.717$ | $2.074$ | $2.508$ | $2.819$ |\n| $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ | $\\cdots$ |\n\nThe rows correspond to different degrees of freedom $k.$ Each cell in the table gives a value $x$ such that \n\n$$\nP(X\\geq x) = p\n$$\n\nfor a particular value of $p.$\n\nSince $X \\sim T_{21},$ we focus on the table row corresponding to $k=21.$\n\n| $k \\setminus p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}0.010}$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $20$ | $1.325$ | $1.725$ | $2.086$ | $2.528$ | $2.845$ |\n| $21$ | $1.323$ | $1.721$ | $2.080$ | $\\bbox[2pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{red}2.518}$ | $2.831$ |\n| $22$ | $1.321$ | $1.717$ | $2.074$ | $2.508$ | $2.819$ |\n\nFrom this row, we see that\n\n$$\nP(X \\geq {\\color{red}2.518}) = {\\color{blue}0.01}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x = 2.518,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/d9/d90b9aec3439d4425f9d8998d7795576f5eb714f5bee93784cd556c18b721bd7.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "20595d2d-9126-5da7-a3b4-8b66e140c084" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6251 :tutorial/title "Working With a Percentage Points Table"} :step/id #uuid "3bfbeda8-b950-5e16-9bf2-bf3182e393e6" :step/math-academy-id 37901 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163060}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163060 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162100 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161666 :question/id #uuid "7898bd86-2b83-5cdd-af38-43e9ac9004e3" :question/math-academy-id "e-10042"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2b7e6407-bb34-5603-b325-580ac32f8a9d" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162366} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162371} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500303} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500328} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500336} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500350}] :knowledge-point/title "Using a Percentage Points Table To Find a T-Score"} :step/id #uuid "ba42cc6b-6af3-5749-a32a-68baeb82fed6" :step/math-academy-id 37902 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163061}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163061 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162101 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161667 :question/id #uuid "86f614ee-dea5-5083-b18b-711f3e2bd84f" :question/math-academy-id "e-10043"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "82f2479b-d8f2-5042-a571-7ba4c713dad8" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049642}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162367} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162376} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500300} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500306} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500315} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500349}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a T-Score Using the Complement"} :step/id #uuid "c66b3235-0142-5eb2-ac43-5b3dfd36c9e5" :step/math-academy-id 37903 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163064}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163062 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163324 :tutorial/content "The shape of the student's $t$-distribution is similar to that of the standard normal distribution. Are they related, and if so, how?\n\nThe two distributions are indeed related. In fact, we have the following theorem:\n\n*The student's $t$-distribution $T_k$ approaches $N(0,1)$ as $k \\to \\infty.$*\n\nThe diagram below shows the student's $t$-distribution for different degrees of freedom $k$ and the standard normal distribution $N(0,1).$ \n\n![](images/94/942080761ebe53527c36102c5e037eb9ee6ef7cb896aca7df130a4992fc6b0d6.png)\n\nNow, as $k\\to\\infty,$ we have that\n\n- the tails of $T_k$ become slimmer,\n\n- the peak of $T_k$ (i.e., the mode) becomes taller, and\n\n- $T_k$ approaches $N(0,1).$" :tutorial/id #uuid "4d267cc0-5208-54de-b750-e18e7728902a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6252 :tutorial/title "Comparing the T-Distribution With the Normal Distribution"} :step/id #uuid "514eead9-fd3a-559f-a1d9-63cf6c58b5a1" :step/math-academy-id 37906} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163064 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162103 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161669 :question/id #uuid "1f694b6c-8ece-5e51-9618-5158e23521fe" :question/math-academy-id "e-10045"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "e6665557-2c04-5082-ba56-28f9376f00e6" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162368} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162370} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500283} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500360} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186500380}] :knowledge-point/title "Computing a T-Score Using Symmetry"} :step/id #uuid "ea119e88-4615-5679-b368-a44c2428c2ef" :step/math-academy-id 37909 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163062}}] :activity/title "The Student's T-Distribution" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162198}]}
